<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-chP-intro-learning-stats" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Learning Statistics is like...</title>
  

  <introduction>
    <p>...taking a general topics course. Data can be found everywhere, and we will look at examples 
      from a variety of disciplines and on a variety of topics. 
   </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
      The best thing about being a statistician is that you get to play in everyone's backyard.
      </p>
     <attribution>
        <line>John Tukey</line>
        <line>Famous Statistician</line>
     </attribution>
   </blockquote>

   <p>Data and data analysis should not exist inside a vacuum, so we
      emphasize the importance of context and how the data connects to real life problems. Don't lose focus of how the data impacts 
      you personally, or your family and community. 
   </p>

  </introduction>



  <subsection xml:id="stats-like-math">
    <title>...Learning Math?</title>
    <p> 
     Yes and No! Statistics <em>is</em> a mathematical science and the methods used by statisticians can involve mathematical computations.
      However, the focus of this book is on developing your "statistical thinking" abilities and your "statistical literacy."
    </p>
      
    <p>  
      Statistical thinking is a type of critical thinking, involving data and <em>inductive reasoning</em>, used to draw reasonable 
      and useful conclusions that can guide decision-making and action <xref ref= "biblio-kaplan-book" />. 

     <aside>
      <title>Inductive Reasoning</title>
      <p>
        is a type of reasoning that involves drawing general conclusions from specific observations.
      </p>
     </aside>
  
     The logic and reasoning used in Statistics may be uncomfortable to you at first. 
     You'll need to learn to think in terms of hypotheticals.

    </p>

    <p> Statistical literacy is the ability to understand, interpret, and critically evaluate data, claims made based on data,
      and other statistical information that is presented to you. 
      This is an important skill to have in today's data-driven world, and is listed as a skill most jobs require.
    </p>
  
    <p>
      Let's start by defining what we mean by data.  
    </p>


    <definition xml:id="def-data">
      <title>Data</title>
      <p> Data is a collection of facts, or information, brought together for reference or analysis and comes in 
        a multitude of formats, including numbers, words, measurements, observations, blocks of text, and images.
      </p>
   </definition> 
    
  </subsection>
    
  
  <subsection xml:id="stats-like-foreign-language">
    <title> ... Learning a Foreign Language? </title>
    <p>
      Yes! There will be a lot of new vocabulary words for you to learn, and we have a specific, precise ways of talking about data 
      and conclusions made from data. This will take practice, and you will need to become fluent in both the vocab and logic in order to be succesful. 
    </p>

    <p>The following is a list of a few of the vocabulary words that you will encounter in this course.
      These are the building blocks of the language of Statistics. 

      <ul>
        <li> Population </li>
        <li> Sample </li>
        <li> Parameter </li>
        <li> Statistic </li>
        <li> Variable </li>
        <li> Distribution </li>
        <li> Random </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p> 
        You will also need to learn the symbols that are used in statistics. For example, the symbol <m>\mu</m> 
        (pronounced: mu) is used to represent a population mean, and <m>\hat{p}</m> (pronounced: p-hat) is used to represent a
        sample proportion. These symbols are not just 
        random letters or characters. They have very specific meanings in the context of Statistics.
        
    </p>
 
    <p> Suggestions for learning the vocabulary: 
      <ul>
        <li> Make flashcards with the word on one side and the definition on the other. </li>
        <li> Use the words in sentences. </li>
        <li> Create mnemonics to help you remember the words. </li>
        <li> Use fun poems/songs to help you remember: <url href="https://www.causeweb.org/cause/resources/fun/">Fun Collection on CauseWeb</url></li>
        <li> Create a word wall in your study space. </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

  </subsection>
 

</section>